====================================================================================================
 MarXus master equation
====================================================================================================
 RUN SETTINGS
 ------------
 Deck:               /home/peter/Dropbox/Research_Leuven/MarXus/validation/c2h3_mess_example/input/c2h3_tight.inp
 Method:             STEADY STATE, OLZMANN (final steady state; steady-state family)
                     J N = R F without an absorbing barrier (Gonzalez-Garcia, Olzmann, PCCP 12, 12290 (2010), eqs. 7, 8)
                     with the thermal rate coefficients of the lowest eigenpair of the same J (eq. 12)
                     and the thermal fates of the wells (Boltzmann distribution of one well as source)
 Solvers:            linear systems: banded Cholesky factorization of the symmetrized J, relative residual <= 1e-8
                     thermal eigenpair: FullDecompositionLapack, sum-rule tolerance 1.5e-2 (warning only)
 Parallel:           4 cores (--ncore); the 40 conditions (T, p) in batches of 4 at a time (rayon)
                     BLAS threads per LAPACK call: 1
 Temperatures (K):   300, 500, 750, 1000, 1250, 1500, 1750, 2000
 Pressures (torr):   76, 228, 760, 2280, 7600
 Energy grid:        grains of 21.000 cm-1 on a common absolute scale, sums of 1 cm-1 counting cells
 Collisions:         exponential down, exactly normalized (Robertson (ed.), CCK 43 (2019), eq. 4.16), transitions up to 15 <dE_down>
                     low-energy reservoirs where eq. 4.16 fails (sparse states, Robertson 2019, p. 294): the grains from the
                       failing one down form one thermalized state (reservoir state, MESMER manual, Sec. 14.2.1):
                       T = 300 K: W1 2 grains (42 cm-1)
                       T = 500 K: W1 2 grains (42 cm-1)
                       T = 750 K: W1 3 grains (63 cm-1)
                       T = 1000 K: W1 4 grains (84 cm-1)
                       T = 1250 K: W1 5 grains (105 cm-1)
                       T = 1500 K: W1 36 grains (756 cm-1)
                       T = 1750 K: W1 40 grains (840 cm-1)
                       T = 2000 K: W1 44 grains (924 cm-1)
                     collision frequency: Lennard-Jones (per well, below)
 Tunneling:          exact Eckart transmission (Miller, J. Am. Chem. Soc. 101, 6810 (1979), eq. 8)
 Source:             thermal reactant P1 through B1 (P1 -> W1)
                     F(E) ~ rho(E) k(E) exp(-E/kT) of the entrance channels (Pfeifle, Olzmann, IJCK 46, 231 (2014), eqs. 7, 9)
====================================================================================================
 CHEMICAL NETWORK
 ----------------

Wells (collisions: <dE_down>(T) = <dE_down>(T_ref) (T/T_ref)^n; Lennard-Jones collision frequency):
      Name   grains    absolute grains   collisions; sink
        W1     2634       -574 .. 2059   <dE_down> = 200 cm-1 (T/300 K)^0.85; sigma 3.455 A, epsilon 64.82 K, reduced mass 3.797 amu

Channels (k(E) of every channel; rate model from the deck):
      Name     From       To   Model
        B1       W1       P1   rigid transition state, Eckart tunneling; entrance of the reactant (source)

Energetics (kcal/mol, relative to P1):

Wells (G - ground energy, D - lowest barrier out of the well):
      Name          G          D
        W1     -34.44       4.42

Bimolecular (G - ground energy):
      Name          G
        P1       0.00

Barriers (H - ZeroEnergy of the barrier in the deck):
      Name          H     From       To   Model
        B1       4.42       W1       P1   rigid transition state, Eckart tunneling

====================================================================================================
Units: unimolecular rate coefficients 1/s; bimolecular rate coefficients cm^3/s; yields in %.
Numbers with six significant digits; *** marks a value that is not available.
====================================================================================================

____________________________________________________________________________________________________
FINAL STEADY STATE (no absorbing barrier)
____________________________________________________________________________________________________

J N = R F without a barrier: under continuous formation, after the stabilized population has reached its own
steady state, the yields of every exit, chemical activation and thermal reaction together (GO10 eqs. 7-9).

Not available: T = 300 K, p = 76 Torr: Banded Cholesky: non-positive pivot -1.920478710408031e9 in row 449; the matrix is not positive definite. The symmetrized operator must be positive definite; in the final steady state this fails when the thermal rate coefficient is negligible compared with the collision frequency (near-singular J); use the intermediate steady state then.
Not available: T = 300 K, p = 228 Torr: Banded Cholesky: non-positive pivot -7.867474513350287e10 in row 458; the matrix is not positive definite. The symmetrized operator must be positive definite; in the final steady state this fails when the thermal rate coefficient is negligible compared with the collision frequency (near-singular J); use the intermediate steady state then.
